Rem ==================================================================
Rem halifax.shoddy — the shell: a prompt, four files, and nothing else.
Rem
Rem     bin/mill run mills/halifax/halifax.shoddy
Rem     ./build.sh run                             (from this directory)
Rem
Rem Everything that touches the outside world is here and nothing else
Rem is: reading a line, printing what came back, and the four words that
Rem name a file. Every decision about what a line MEANS is in
Rem halifax-core.shoddy, which is pure and which test.shoddy grades with
Rem no terminal anywhere near it.
Rem
Rem ---- why the loop is one Def -------------------------------------
Rem
Rem HxLoop calls itself and only itself, in tail position. The compiler
Rem turns a SELF tail call into a jump and a mutual one into a call, so a
Rem loop written as HxLoop -> HxTurn -> HxLoop would grow the stack by a
Rem frame or two per line entered. That is fine for an afternoon and not
Rem fine for a redirected script of a hundred thousand lines, and
Rem "nothing keyable aborts the mill" does not have an exception for
Rem typing a lot. So QUIT is decided by a pure test BEFORE the turn
Rem rather than by the turn handing back a stop, and the price is reading
Rem the line twice, which costs a tokenize.
Rem
Rem ---- what QUIT does not do ---------------------------------------
Rem
Rem Nothing is saved on the way out. The tape and the words you defined
Rem are gone unless SAVE or TAPESAVE put them somewhere, which is what
Rem HELP QUIT says. A calculator that wrote files as it closed would be a
Rem calculator that wrote files you did not ask for.
Rem
Rem ---- paths -------------------------------------------------------
Rem
Rem Resolved against the CURRENT DIRECTORY, and halifaxrc is looked for
Rem there too. Guarded twins throughout — TryReadFile and TryWriteFile,
Rem never ReadFile or WriteFile — because an unwritable path is an
Rem ordinary answer to give at a prompt and an aborted mill is not.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "../../machines/str.shoddy"
Include "halifax-core.shoddy"

Rem ---- reading a line --------------------------------------------------

Rem End of input is not an empty line, and the difference is the whole
Rem reason this is InputLine and not Input: a redirected script has to end
Rem the session, and a blank line typed at a prompt has to earn a fresh
Rem prompt. InputLine reports the two separately; this lifts its flat
Rem carrier into an answer that can be matched.
Type HxIn = HxEof | HxTyped(HxText As String)

Def HxAsked() As HxIn
    HxAskedOf(InputLine(HxPrompt()))

Def HxAskedOf(r As Array) As HxIn
    If Nth(r, 1) Then
        HxEof()
    Else
        HxTyped(Nth(r, 2))

Rem ---- the loop --------------------------------------------------------

Def HxLoop(st As RckState)
    Select Case HxAsked()
        Case HxTyped(line)
            If HxQuits(line) Then
                Print("")
            Else
                HxLoop(HxTurn(st, line))
        Case Else
            Print("")

Rem One turn: work out what the line was, do it, show it, and hand back
Rem the state to go on from. A refusal is a state too — the one the line
Rem started from, with the refusal on the tape.
Def HxTurn(st As RckState, line As String) As RckState
    HxShowed(st, HxActed(st, line))

Def HxShowed(st As RckState, r As RckLine) As RckState
    Each(HxShown(r), Print)
    HxAfter(st, r)

Def HxActed(st As RckState, line As String) As RckLine
    Select Case HxRead(line)
        Case HxEval(l)
            HxDo(st, l)
        Case HxPath(w, p)
            HxFiled(st, line, w, p)
        Case HxPlain(w)
            HxPlained(st, line, w)
        Case HxAskBad(why)
            HxRefused(st, line, why)
        Case Else
            HxRefused(st, line, "the line did not read")

Rem RESET is the one intercepted word that performs no effect: a fresh
Rem state, which only this mill can build because only this mill knows
Rem which seeds went into the last one. It is not taped, for the same
Rem reason CLEARTAPE is not — a line that empties the tape cannot leave
Rem its own record on the empty tape.
Def HxPlained(st As RckState, line As String, w As String) As RckLine
    If w = HxResetWord() Then
        RckNext(HxFresh(), { "reset" })
    Else
        HxRefused(st, line, w & " is decided before the turn and should never arrive here")

Rem ---- the four words that name a file ---------------------------------

Def HxFiled(st As RckState, line As String, w As String, path As String) As RckLine
    If w = HxSaveWord() Then
        HxSavedWords(st, line, path)
    Else
        If w = HxTapeSaveWord() Then
            HxSavedTape(st, line, path)
        Else
            HxLoaded(st, line, path)

Def HxSavedWords(st As RckState, line As String, path As String) As RckLine
    If TryWriteFile(path, HxFileText(RckSave(st))) Then
        HxSaid(st, line, "saved " & HxCounted(HxUserCount(st), "word", "words"))
    Else
        HxRefused(st, line, "cannot write " & path)

Rem The tape is written as it stands, which does not include the TAPESAVE
Rem line itself: a line cannot record having done something it has not
Rem finished doing. The confirmation goes on the tape afterwards, so the
Rem next TAPESAVE writes this one.
Def HxSavedTape(st As RckState, line As String, path As String) As RckLine
    If TryWriteFile(path, HxFileText(RckTape(st))) Then
        HxSaid(st, line, "wrote " & HxCounted(Length(RckTape(st)), "row", "rows"))
    Else
        HxRefused(st, line, "cannot write " & path)

Rem The missing file is asked about separately rather than left to
Rem TryReadFile's own reason, which is shouted upper case the way the
Rem runtime's messages are. Every refusal the engine gives is lower-case
Rem prose, and a prompt that answers in two voices reads as though one of
Rem them is a bug. FileExists covers the case that happens; the guarded
Rem read still covers the ones that do not — a directory, a file being
Rem written, a permission that is not there.
Def HxLoaded(st As RckState, line As String, path As String) As RckLine
    If Not FileExists(path) Then
        HxRefused(st, line, "there is no file called " & path)
    Else
        HxLoadedRead(st, line, path, TryReadFile(path))

Def HxLoadedRead(st As RckState, line As String, path As String, r As Result) As RckLine
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(text)
            HxLoadedInto(st, line, RckLoad(st, HxTextLines(text)))
        Case Else
            HxRefused(st, line, "cannot read " & path)

Rem A file with one bad line in it changes nothing: RckLoad validates a
Rem saved line exactly as a typed one and refuses the whole file, so the
Rem dictionary is never left half-populated by a truncated download.
Def HxLoadedInto(st As RckState, line As String, r As RckLine) As RckLine
    Select Case r
        Case RckNext(st2, out)
            HxLoadedShown(st, st2, line, out)
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            HxRefused(st, line, why)
        Case Else
            HxRefused(st, line, "the file did not load")

Rem Every loaded line gets the same row a typed one would have — you see
Rem what LOAD did, not just how many words it left behind. RckLoad built
Rem this already; the summary is the one thing this mill still owns.
Def HxLoadedShown(st As RckState, st2 As RckState, line As String, out As List Of String) As RckLine
    Let said = Append(out, "loaded " & HxCounted(HxUserCount(st2) - HxUserCount(st), "word", "words"))
    RckNext(RckTapedOk(st, st2, line, said), said)

Rem ---- text and files --------------------------------------------------

Rem One line per row and a closing newline, so the file ends the way every
Rem other text file does and a shell that cats it gets its prompt back on
Rem a line of its own. An empty tape writes an empty file rather than a
Rem file containing one blank line.
Def HxFileText(rows As List Of String) As String
    If IsEmpty(rows) Then
        ""
    Else
        Join(rows, Chr(10)) & Chr(10)

Rem Split on the newline and Trim what is left, which takes the carriage
Rem return off a file written on Windows and read on anything else. A
Rem definition's leading spaces mean nothing to the tokenizer, so there is
Rem nothing here for Trim to damage.
Def HxTextLines(text As String) As List Of String
    Map(Split(text, Chr(10)), Trim)

Rem ---- starting up -----------------------------------------------------

Def HxRcName() As String
    "halifaxrc"

Rem A missing halifaxrc is silence, not an error: most sessions have no
Rem such file and a mill that complained about its absence would be
Rem complaining at everyone. A file that IS there and will not load says
Rem so and starts anyway, because a bad startup file is a reason to fix
Rem the file and not a reason to have no calculator.
Rem
Rem It loads through RckLoad rather than through a turn, so it leaves no
Rem tape rows: the tape is a record of a session, and nobody typed this.
Def HxStart(st As RckState) As RckState
    If Not FileExists(HxRcName()) Then
        st
    Else
        HxStartRead(st, TryReadFile(HxRcName()))

Def HxStartRead(st As RckState, r As Result) As RckState
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(text)
            HxStartLoad(st, RckLoad(st, HxTextLines(text)))
        Case Else
            HxStartSaid(st, HxRcName() & ": cannot read it")

Def HxStartLoad(st As RckState, r As RckLine) As RckState
    Select Case r
        Case RckNext(st2, out)
            HxStartSaid(st2, HxRcName() & ": loaded " & HxCounted(HxUserCount(st2) - HxUserCount(st), "word", "words"))
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            HxStartSaid(st, HxRcName() & ": " & why)
        Case Else
            st

Def HxStartSaid(st As RckState, said As String) As RckState
    Print(said)
    st

Rem ---- the run ---------------------------------------------------------

Rem The banner, then the startup file, then the stack — and only then the
Rem first prompt. The stack is printed once here and once per turn after
Rem that, which is why an empty session opens on [ empty ] rather than on
Rem nothing at all.
Def HxOpened() As RckState
    Let st = HxStart(HxFresh())
    Each(RckShow(st), Print)
    st

Def Main()
    Each(HxBanner(), Print)
    HxLoop(HxOpened())
